    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Math!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="4" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Participate in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher. reading_2: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids. phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize basic familiar words related to personal preferences (e.g., 'math' or 'like') when supported with illustrations or visual aids (e.g., recognizing the word 'math' while looking at a picture of numbers). Can participate in choral reading by recognizing and producing the beginning sounds of familiar words related to personal preferences, such as 'science,' 'music,' or 'art' (e.g., identifying and producing the /s/ sound for the letter 'S' in 'science' during group reading). Can recognize familiar letters in words related to personal preferences, such as 'math' and 'like' (e.g., recognizing the letter 'M' in 'math' and the letter 'L' in 'like' when supported with pictures).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge, and introduce the target language through modeling and clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows flashcards of school subjects (math, art, science, music) and activities (games, playground) from Lesson 1.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says "I like math!" and points to the 'math' flashcard. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then shows the written word "math" (on a separate card or below the picture) and says "This is 'math'." Students repeat chorally, pointing to the word. Repeat with 'like' (using a picture of a smiling face/heart) and 'games' (picture of children playing). This connects spoken words to their written form.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models pointing to the written word while saying it clearly, emphasizing the beginning sounds (e.g., /m/ in 'math', /l/ in 'like', /g/ in 'games').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher runs a finger under the word 'math' as she says "math," demonstrating how to follow the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher also models expressing preferences with facial expressions and simple gestures: "I like math!" (thumbs up, smile) and "I don't like..." (thumbs down, slight frown – though the focus is on likes today).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the written word "math" next to its picture): "Is this 'math'?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the written word "like" next to its picture): "Do you see the word 'like'?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up the 'science' flashcard, then pointing to the written word 'math'): "Is this 'science'?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">New language or content is introduced within the "I Like School!" scenario, providing authentic input for reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Like School!":</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher presents a large, colorful chart with a simple, illustrated "I Like School!" chant/poem. The text will include familiar vocabulary words like 'math', 'games', 'science', 'music', 'art', and the phrase 'I like...'. Each line will be paired with a clear illustration.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example text:
                        <br><i>I like school. (Picture of school)
                        <br>I like math. (Picture of numbers)
                        <br>I like games. (Picture of kids playing)
                        <br>I like science. (Picture of a test tube/plant)
                        <br>I like music. (Picture of a musical note)
                        <br>I like art. (Picture of crayons/drawing)</i>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Genre: Simple chant/poem. Register: Informal. Format: Large illustrated chart or big book with clear, large print.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizing basic familiar words related to personal preferences (e.g., 'math', 'like') when supported with illustrations or visual aids. Participating in choral reading.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the chant aloud clearly, pointing to each word as she reads. Students listen and follow along.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher rereads, and students point to the correct picture on the chart when they hear a specific word (e.g., "Point to 'math'!" "Point to 'games'!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks simple gist questions: "Does the boy like school?" (Students nod "yes"). "Does he like math?" (Students nod "yes").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the word 'like' in different lines and asks: "What word is this?" (Expected: "Like!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in reading through controlled and semi-controlled activities, reinforcing target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Subject + verb ('I like school.'), Present simple ('I like games.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: 'math', 'like', 'games', 'science', 'music', 'art'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Phonemic Awareness: Recognizing familiar letters, specifically 'M' in 'math', 'L' in 'like', 'S' in 'science', 'G' in 'games'.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Word-to-Picture Match (Controlled Practice)</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a worksheet with pictures of school subjects (math, art, music, science) on one side and the corresponding words written out on the other side.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher models: "Draw a line from 'math' to the picture of numbers."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw lines to match each word to its picture. Teacher circulates and provides individual support.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Letter Recognition Circle (Semi-controlled)</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher writes a few target words (e.g., MATH, LIKE, GAMES, SCIENCE, MUSIC) largely on the board or on individual cards.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the letter 'M' in 'MATH' and says, "This is 'M'. Can you find 'M'?" Students point to the letter.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then receive a short list of words (e.g., math, music, play, like, sing, game) and are asked to "Circle all the 'M's you see!" or "Circle all the 'L's!" focusing on specific target letters.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The class does a quick choral phonemic awareness drill for /m/, /l/, /s/, /g/ by saying the sound as they see the letter in the word.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Choral Reading with Finger Tracking</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students revisit the "I Like School!" chant/poem from Stage 2.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher leads the class in choral reading, line by line. Students are encouraged to use their fingers to track the words as they read along.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher pauses at key vocabulary words (e.g., 'math', 'like', 'games') and points to them, asking students to say the word aloud again with emphasis.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the target language in a more realistic and communicative reading task within the "I Like School!" scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Reading Hunt" – collaborative word and letter identification.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher prepares simplified mini-charts or large print-outs of sentences related to the theme "I Like Math!" and "I Like School!" (e.g., "I like math." "I like games." "Do you like art?"). These will have illustrations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs or small groups (2-3 students). Each group receives a mini-chart/print-out and a marker.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity: "Find the Word!"</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher calls out a target word, "Find 'math'!" The groups work together to find the word 'math' on their chart and circle it.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher repeats for other target words: 'like', 'games', 'art', 'science', 'music'.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity: "Find the Letter!"</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher calls out a target letter, "Find 'M' in 'math'!" The groups identify the letter 'M' in the word 'math' and underline it.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher repeats for other letters/words: 'L' in 'like', 'G' in 'games', 'S' in 'science'.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, observes group collaboration, and provides assistance or affirmation ("Good job finding 'like'!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategies are used to check student learning, followed by a brief student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Individually, students are shown 2-3 flashcards each containing a word (e.g., 'math', 'like', 'games') and its corresponding picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks each student to point to the word 'math' or 'like'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a letter (e.g., 'M' in 'math') and asks, "What letter is this?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher observes participation in the final choral reading session of the "I Like School!" chant.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can recognize and point to the words 'math' and 'like' when supported by a picture (reading_2).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can recognize familiar letters 'M', 'L', 'S', or 'G' within a word (phonemic_awareness).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates actively in choral reading sessions (reading_1).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are given a paper with two simple sentences: "I like math!" and "I like games!" with corresponding pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw a happy face next to the sentence if they *can* find and recognize the word 'math' and 'games'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning process and plan for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word you can find in our 'I Like School!' chart?" (Teacher points to the chart, students can point or attempt to say).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you like to read about school?" (Students respond with "Yes!" or thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students recognize words and letters? (e.g., flashcards, finger tracking, choral reading).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Speaking)? (e.g., more practice with 'I like...' sentence structure for oral production, more opportunities for individual student responses).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the tasks align well with Kinder A1.2 reading skills (recognizing, pointing, choral participation)?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Today, students recognized words like 'math' and 'games' in text. In Lesson 3 (Speaking), they will actively use these words to express their own preferences, saying "I like math!" or "I like games!" and asking "Do you like it?" in simple communicative activities.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>